    {/* ═════════════════════════ L2 — MERTENS ═══════════════════════════════ */}
    <section data-min-depth="L2" class="mb-14">
      <span class="pt-tag pt-tag-der mb-3 inline-block">Standard</span>
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-thmblue mb-4">
        Mertens: the rate at which survivors thin out<span class="inline-flex items-center px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-wider bg-slate-200 text-slate-700 ml-2 align-middle">classical</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[1fr_0.9fr] gap-8 items-start">
        <div class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed space-y-4">
          <p>
            How many integers survive a sieve of depth $y$? The naive
            density is the truncated Eulerian product:
          </p>
          <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-4 font-mono text-center">
            {String.raw`$V(y) = \prod_{p \leq y} \left(1 - \dfrac{1}{p}\right)$`}
          </div>
          <p>
            Mertens (1874) proved the exact asymptotic:
          </p>
          <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-4 font-mono text-center">
            {String.raw`$V(y) \sim \dfrac{e^{-\gamma}}{\log y}$`}
          </div>
          <p>
            where {String.raw`$\gamma \approx 0.5772$`} is the
            <strong>Euler–Mascheroni constant</strong>. The factor
            {String.raw`$e^{-\gamma} \approx 0.5615$`} appears everywhere in
            sieves — it is the fundamental proportionality coefficient
            between naive and observed density.
          </p>
          <p>
            For PT, $e^\gamma$ is exactly the prefactor of the universal
            kernel {String.raw`$K_{PT}(u) = e^\gamma \omega(u)$`} (see L3
            below, PT mathematical programme).
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-5">
          <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue mb-3">Survival density in practice</h3>
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-300">
                <th class="text-left py-2">$y$</th>
                <th class="text-right py-2">$V(y)$</th>
                <th class="text-right py-2">{String.raw`$e^{-\gamma}/\log y$`}</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="font-mono text-xs">
              <tr><td>10</td><td class="text-right">0.2295</td><td class="text-right">0.2438</td></tr>
              <tr><td>100</td><td class="text-right">0.1203</td><td class="text-right">0.1219</td></tr>
              <tr><td>1,000</td><td class="text-right">0.0813</td><td class="text-right">0.0813</td></tr>
              <tr><td>10,000</td><td class="text-right">0.0610</td><td class="text-right">0.0610</td></tr>
              <tr><td>100,000</td><td class="text-right">0.0488</td><td class="text-right">0.0488</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 mt-3 italic">
            Convergence is fast: at $y = 1000$, the relative error is
            already below 0.1%.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ═════════════════════════ L2 — DISTRIBUTION π(x) ═════════════════════ */}
    <section data-min-depth="L2" class="mb-14">
      <span class="pt-tag pt-tag-der mb-3 inline-block">Standard</span>
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-thmblue mb-4">
        Distribution π(x): the Prime Number Theorem<span class="inline-flex items-center px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-wider bg-slate-200 text-slate-700 ml-2 align-middle">classical</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[0.8fr_1.2fr] gap-8 items-start">
        <div class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed space-y-4">
          <p>
            $\pi(x) = $ number of primes $\leq x$. The <strong>Prime
            Number Theorem</strong> (Hadamard, de la Vallée Poussin, 1896)
            establishes that:
          </p>
          <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-4 font-mono text-center">
            {String.raw`$\pi(x) \sim \dfrac{x}{\log x}$`}
          </div>
          <p>
            A much sharper approximation is given by the <strong>logarithmic
            integral</strong>:
          </p>
          <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-4 font-mono text-center text-sm">
            {String.raw`$\mathrm{Li}(x) = \int_2^x \dfrac{dt}{\log t}$`}
          </div>
          <p>
            The discrepancy {String.raw`$\pi(x) - \mathrm{Li}(x)$`} changes
            sign infinitely often (Littlewood, 1914), even though numerically
            {String.raw`$\pi(x) < \mathrm{Li}(x)$`} for every $x$ up to the
            first crossover, estimated around {String.raw`$10^{316}$`}
            (Skewes' number).
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-5">
          <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue mb-3">π(x), x/log(x), Li(x) — exact values</h3>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 760 360" class="w-full h-auto" role="img" aria-label="Distribution of pi(x)">
            <rect x="0" y="0" width="760" height="360" fill="#fbfdff" stroke="#cbd5e1" rx="6" />
            <g transform="translate(70, 30)">
              {/* axes */}
