              </text>
            </g>
          </svg>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ═════════════════════════ L2 — SQUARE-ROOT WINDOW ════════════════════ */}
    <section data-min-depth="L2" class="mb-14">
      <span class="pt-tag pt-tag-der mb-3 inline-block">Standard</span>
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-thmblue mb-4">
        The key point: the square-root window<span class="inline-flex items-center px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-wider bg-slate-200 text-slate-700 ml-2 align-middle">classical</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[1fr_0.9fr] gap-6 items-start">
        <div class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed space-y-4">
          <p>
            Let $S(x;y)$ denote the number of integers $n \leq x$ divisible
            by no prime $\leq y$. If we choose
            {String.raw`$y = \lfloor\sqrt{x}\rfloor$`}, then no composite
            can survive: it necessarily has a prime divisor
            {String.raw`$\leq \sqrt{n} \leq \sqrt{x}$`}.
          </p>
          <p>
            The survivors are therefore exactly 1 and the primes in the
            interval {String.raw`$(\sqrt{x}, x]$`}. We obtain:
          </p>
          <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-4 font-mono text-center text-sm">
            {String.raw`$\pi(x) = S(x; \lfloor\sqrt{x}\rfloor) + \pi(\lfloor\sqrt{x}\rfloor) - 1$`}
          </div>
          <p>
            This formula changes the problem: understanding the
            distribution of primes amounts to understanding a
            <strong>finite-window survival law</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-5">
          <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue mb-3">What PT shifts</h3>
          <ol class="space-y-3 text-sm text-slate-700">
            <li><strong>1.</strong> First build the sieve survivors.</li>
            <li><strong>2.</strong> Read their gaps as persistence gaps.</li>
            <li><strong>3.</strong> Move to the {String.raw`$\sqrt{x}$`} window.</li>
            <li><strong>4.</strong> Recover primes as ultimate survivors.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ═════════════════════════ L2 — WHEEL MOD 30 ══════════════════════════ */}
    <section data-min-depth="L2" class="mb-14">
      <span class="pt-tag pt-tag-der mb-3 inline-block">Standard</span>
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-thmblue mb-4">
        Primorial wheels: why 30, 210, 2310…<span class="inline-flex items-center px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-wider bg-slate-200 text-slate-700 ml-2 align-middle">classical</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed space-y-4 max-w-3xl">
        <p>
          To enumerate primes efficiently, one does not work with the
          integers one by one. One uses a <strong>wheel</strong>: a cycle
          of residues coprime to a primorial
          $p_k\# = 2 \cdot 3 \cdot 5 \cdots p_k$.
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
          <li>Wheel mod 6 ({String.raw`$2 \cdot 3$`}): 2 residues {`{1, 5}`}, period 6.</li>
          <li>Wheel mod 30 ({String.raw`$2 \cdot 3 \cdot 5$`}): 8 residues {`{1, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, 23, 29}`}, period 30.</li>
          <li>Wheel mod 210 ({String.raw`$2 \cdot 3 \cdot 5 \cdot 7$`}): 48 residues, period 210.</li>
          <li>Wheel mod 2310: 480 residues, period 2310.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The number of residues is $\varphi(p_k\#) = p_k\# \prod (1 - 1/p)$.
          The larger the wheel, the lower the survivor density (and the
          more efficient each step).
        </p>
        <p>
          For PT, these wheels are the <strong>arithmetic tori</strong>
          {String.raw`$\mathbb{T}^k = \mathbb{Z}/p_1 \times \cdots \times \mathbb{Z}/p_k$`}
          via the Chinese remainder theorem (CRT). The active PT torus is
          {String.raw`$\mathbb{T}^3 = \mathbb{Z}/3 \times \mathbb{Z}/5 \times \mathbb{Z}/7$`}
          — precisely the wheel mod 105 — and underpins the attractor
          $\mu^* = 15$.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ═════════════════════════ L2 — BRIDGE WITH PT (T0) ═══════════════════ */}
    <section data-min-depth="L2" class="mb-14">
