        Exact angle law: θ_p ~ √(2/p)<span class="inline-flex items-center px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-wider bg-thmblue/10 text-thmblue ml-2 align-middle">PT contribution</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1fr] gap-8 items-start">
        <div class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed space-y-4">
          <p>
            By placing each prime $p$ on the PT circle through its holonomy
            angle $\theta_p$, one discovers an <strong>exact law</strong>:
          </p>
          <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded p-3 font-mono text-center">
            {String.raw`$\theta_p \sim \sqrt{2/p}, \qquad p\,\theta_p^2/2 \to 1$`}
          </div>
          <p>
            <strong>Consequence on angular gaps.</strong> For two primes
            {String.raw`$p < q$`}:
          </p>
          <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded p-3 font-mono text-center text-sm">
            {String.raw`$\theta_p - \theta_q \sim \sqrt{2/p} - \sqrt{2/q}$`}
          </div>
          <p>
            For <strong>adjacent</strong> primes
            {String.raw`$p < p_{n+1}$`}:
          </p>
          <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded p-3 font-mono text-center text-sm">
            {String.raw`$\Delta\theta(p, p_{n+1}) \sim \dfrac{p_{n+1} - p}{\sqrt{2}\, p^{3/2}}$`}
          </div>
          <p>
            Reading: <em>the PT circle turns prime gaps into much smaller
            angular gaps, by a rigid scaling law in
            {String.raw`$p^{-3/2}$`}</em>. No second independent random law
            appears.
          </p>
          <p>
            Numerical validation: for the differential square root, the
            observed median ratio is {String.raw`$1.00009$`} for $p \geq 997$.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-5">
          <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue mb-3 text-sm">
